# Raw survey deliverables, gathered under surveys/ on 19 Sep 2026.
#
# They used to be one folder per delivery at the repository root, named however
# they arrived -- `Aektanagar/` holding `Aekatanagar DSM.tif`, two different
# misspellings of Ektanagar in one path; a lowercase `ektanagar/` that was
# actually Ektanagar *2*; Kotba's files spread across `DSM/`, `DTM/`,
# `Contours/` and two loose orthomosaics at the root. Each new delivery needed
# its own ignore rule, and twice one arrived without one and sat untracked and
# unignored, a single `git add -A` away from staging tens of gigabytes.
#
# One rule now, and one place. Folder names here are for humans; the site slugs
# they correspond to are fixed by R2 keys and are not the same strings
# (`surveys/ektanagar-1` is `aektanagar-survey` in the bucket).
/surveys/

# Reference material: Malhar's tool specs and hydrology samples, the request
# documents behind the tool improvements, and raw screenshots. Inputs to the
# work rather than anything the site ships; processed copies live in public/.
/reference/

# Anything raw left at the root, which should now be nothing.
/*.ecw
/*.tif
/*.tiff
*.zip

# reference / demo videos (large, kept locally only)
*.mp4

# Client portal logins. Nothing writes this any more — password sign in was
# removed on 18 Sep 2026 and Google is the only route in — but the ignore stays
# so a leftover file on someone's machine cannot be committed by a `git add -A`.
/portal-data/users.json

# iCloud sync conflict copies. This project lives in ~/Documents, which syncs,
# and a conflict leaves a "<name> 2.ts" beside the original. 35 of them were
# committed once, including stale copies of the auth and session code.
#
# The second pattern catches directories and extensionless files, which the first
# one misses because it requires a dot: "src/components/portal/admin 2" appeared
# on 26 Jul 2026 and was not ignored. iCloud also does this inside .git, where
# gitignore cannot help: a stray "refs/heads/<branch> 2" makes git fetch fail
# with "bad object", and git hides it as a broken ref name. Delete those by hand.
* [0-9].*
* [0-9]

# Tile pyramids ARE committed, because the portal serves the map from them and
# they have to reach Vercel. Do not add an ignore rule here: the layer route only
# serves a tile the manifest declares, so an ignored pyramid is a 404 on a
# deployed site with nothing wrong locally.
#
# Reproduce them with scripts/prepare-site.mjs, or export from QGIS/GDAL on the
# machine that holds the survey (context.md 8j and 8k). They stay small because
# empty tiles are never written: about 1 MB for Kotba, 17 MB for Aektanagar. Once
# a site passes the 50 MB trigger in 8h, that site moves to object storage and
# gets ignored here instead.

# Source terrain models the analysis API reads, symlinked to the gitignored
# survey folders above. Local only: in production these come from object storage
# via PORTAL_TERRAIN_DIR, and committing the symlinks would dangle on Vercel.
# The map bundles left the repository on 19 Sep 2026.
#
# They were tracked because there was no other way to get them to production:
# the portal read them from process.cwd(), so shipping a survey meant committing
# its rasters. That is the repository acting as a CDN — 156 MB of client data in
# the pack — and it made publishing impossible to hand to anyone else, because
# the last step was a commit.
#
# PORTAL_MAP_URL ended that. R2 is where production reads them from and where
# they are backed up; upload-site.mjs pushes them from this directory, which is
# now a working copy rather than the canonical one. Keep them locally, publish
# them with `--only map`, and do not commit them.
/portal-data/map/

# portal-data/files/ is still tracked, and deliberately so: `files.ts` reads it
# from disk with no URL alternative and upload-site has no class for it, so a
# deliverable that is not committed cannot be downloaded in production. That is
# the same migration this one just made, not yet done — until it is, these 21 MB
# have to ship inside the deployment.
#
# Test sites publish for free under these slugs, which is how eleven fixture
# tiles reached a commit on 18 Sep 2026.
/portal-data/files/*/fixture-site/
/portal-data/files/*/test-*/

/portal-data/terrain/

# Wrangler's local state for the tile Worker: build output and a dev cache,
# rewritten on every deploy and specific to one machine's login.
/.wrangler/

# Derived hydrology layers, written by scripts/hydro-run.mjs from the gitignored
# terrain above. Regenerable from one command, several megabytes per site, and
# in production they come from object storage like everything else.
/portal-data/hydrology/

# Point cloud quadtrees, written by scripts/prepare-point-cloud.mjs from the
# gitignored LAS above. 126 MB for Aektanagar alone, regenerable from one
# command, and in production they come from object storage like the rest.
/portal-data/cloud/

# Forest inventory artefacts, written by scripts/forest-run.mjs. Same shape as
# hydrology: regenerable from one command, tens of megabytes per site (a crown
# polygon file alone is over 100 MB for Ektanagar 1), and served from object
# storage in production via PORTAL_FOREST_URL.
/portal-data/forest/

# pytorch-lightning writes this next to wherever a DeepForest inference is
# run from (scripts/forest-detect.py) -- hyperparameter logs from a run that
# trained nothing, pure noise.
/lightning_logs/
